# Tessera stdlib: B128 tan, correctly rounded
#
# CORE-MATH has no binary128 tan, so tan runs in three tiers:
#
# - quick: CORE-MATH's sine and cosine pipeline (TrigShared.te) reduces |x| once and gives sin |x| and cos |x|
#   as normalized fractions, and tan is their quotient, divided exactly in fixed point and rounded here unless it
#   lies too close to a rounding midpoint. The fractions come from the 128-bit fast path, or from the 384-bit
#   accurate path when the fast reduction keeps too few bits (x near a multiple of π/256). On random arguments
#   about 0.4% go on.
# - fast: |x| = k π/2 + θ by b128_reduce, then sin θ / cos θ (k even) or -cos θ / sin θ (k odd) in B128Extended.
#   The result is kept when all of it × (1 ± 2^-B128_TAN_BAND) rounds to the same B128.
# - exact: otherwise the same formulas in B128Big (448 bits, Big.te), rounded once.
#
# The quick tier's bounds are proven. CORE-MATH's sinq_error.md bounds each fast-path fraction (normalized into
# [2^127, 2^128) units of 2^(e - 128)) within 15.8 units of the exact sin or cos, so each is off by a relative α
# or β with |α|, |β| < 15.8 / (2^127 - 15.8). Their quotient s / c (in [1/2, 2)) is scaled into [2^127, 2^128)
# by 2^128 or 2^127 and divided exactly, keeping the floor. With S = s (1 + α) and C = c (1 + β), the scaled
# quotient Q is off from the exact one by Q |α - β| / (1 + α) < 2^128 × 31.6 / (2^127 - 31.6) < 63.3 units, and
# the floor by less than one more: under 64.3 units in all. So the 15 bits below the 113-bit significand are
# rounded only when they lie more than B128_TAN_GATE = 64 units from the midpoint 2^14. The exact tan then lies on
# the same side of the midpoint, or in the same rounding cell at a binade's edge. Everything nearer goes on.
# The accurate path's fractions are good to far better than 2^-280 relative (its 448-bit reduction keeps over 300
# bits, since no B128 comes within 2^-124 of a multiple of π/2, and its series are within 2^-296), and cutting
# each to its top 128 bits adds under one unit: |α|, |β| < 2^-127 + 2^-280, so Q is within 5.01 units and
# B128_TAN_WIDE_GATE = 8 decides there.
#
# The band is measured, not proven: on about 110000 inputs (every exponent, tiny ones, 1/2 to 2, near k π/4 and
# k π/2 for small and huge k, up to B128.MAX) the fast tier's worst relative error against mpmath was 2^-150.2, at
# |θ| near π/4 where the sin and cos series stop, so 2^-145 leaves about 5 bits. Results near a rounding boundary do
# occur: just above 2^-57, tan x - x = x³/3 + … sweeps past the midpoints between B128s so slowly that the nearest
# x lands within about 2^-225 of one, and the fast tier alone rounds some of those the wrong way
# (tests/b128_tan.te has them).
#
# b128_reduce keeps 256 bits of x × 2/π's fraction, so θ is good to about 2^-256 absolute, and tan's relative error
# grows as θ shrinks. Past |x| = 1/2, a θ under 2^-90 therefore goes straight to the exact tier (the reduction
# alone would leave 2^-166 relative there). The closest a B128 gets to a multiple of π/2 is |θ| = 2^-123.3, found
# by continued fractions of 2^e × 2/π for every exponent: x = 8794873135033829349702184924722639 × 2^1852, whose
# tan is about 1.27e37. big_reduce's θ is good to 2^-449 absolute, so even there the exact tier keeps 2^-325
# relative.
#
# The only exact result is tan(±0) = ±0: for a rational x ≠ 0, e^(2ix) is transcendental (Lindemann), so tan x =
# -i (e^(2ix) - 1) / (e^(2ix) + 1) is too, and it is never a B128 or a midpoint between two. Below |x| = 2^-57 the
# answer is x itself: tan x - x < x³/2 < 2^-115 |x|, under half an ulp of x (at least 2^-114 |x|), and tan x lies
# on x's side away from zero.

module Standard/System/Numerics/B128

### The fast tier's error band for tan, as a power of two: a B128Extended result is kept only when all of it × (1 ± 2^-145) rounds to the same B128.
### :note: Measured, not proven: the worst fast-tier error seen against mpmath was about 2^-150.2, so the band leaves about 5 bits.
preset B128_TAN_BAND: S64 = 145

### The quick tier's refusal window: it rounds only when the 15 guard bits lie more than this many units from the midpoint.
### :note: Proven: the quick quotient is within 64.3 units of the exact one (the derivation is at the top of this file).
preset B128_TAN_GATE: U64 = 64

### The quick tier's refusal window when it divides the accurate path's 384-bit sine and cosine, cut to 128 bits.
### :note: Proven: that quotient is within 5.01 units of the exact one, and 8 leaves room.
preset B128_TAN_WIDE_GATE: U64 = 8

### Past |x| = 1/2, the fast tier needs |θ| >= 2^-B128_TAN_LOOSE (else b128_reduce's 2^-256 is too coarse).
preset B128_TAN_LOOSE: S64 = 90

### tan x, correctly rounded (round to nearest, ties to even): ±0 for ±0, a quiet NaN for NaN, NaN for infinities.
### :returns: tan(`me`).
routine B128.tan() -> Me
    r: Me = b128_tan(x: me, exact: false)
    return r

### tan x, correctly rounded, as {B128.tan}. Below |x| = 2^-57 the answer is x itself.
### :param x: The argument.
### :param exact: True skips the quick and fast tiers (the tests use it to check the exact tier on its own).
### :returns: tan x.
routine b128_tan(x: B128, exact: Bool) -> B128
    bits: U128 = x.bits()
    is_nan: Bool = b128_is_nan_bits(bits)
    infinite: Bool = x.is_infinite()
    # |x| < 2^-57, zeros too
    when
        is_nan => return b128_of(b128_quiet(bits))
        infinite => return nan
        b128_exponent(bits) < B128_BIAS.sub(57) => return x
        else =>
            r: B128 = b128_tan_general(x, exact)
            return r

### tan x for a finite x with |x| >= 2^-57, by the three tiers.
### :param x: The argument.
### :param exact: True skips the quick and fast tiers.
### :returns: tan x, correctly rounded.
secret routine b128_tan_general(x: B128, exact: Bool) -> B128
    if not exact
        q: Maybe[U128] = cm_tan_quick(x.bits())
        when q
            is U128 as bits => return b128_of(bits)
            is None =>
                red: Reduced = b128_reduce(x)
                if not wide_theta_is_loose(x, theta: red.theta)
                    w: B128Extended = wide_tan(x, red)
                    rounded: WideRounding = wide_round_within(u: w, k: B128_TAN_BAND)
                    if rounded.ok
                        return rounded.value
    r: B128 = big_tan(x)
    return r

### Whether b128_reduce's θ is too coarse for the fast tier: |x| >= 1/2 (below it θ is |x| exactly) and θ is
### zero or under 2^-B128_TAN_LOOSE.
### :param x: The argument.
### :param theta: θ from {b128_reduce}.
### :returns: True when the exact tier must run.
secret routine wide_theta_is_loose(x: B128, theta: B128Extended) -> Bool
    reduced: Bool = b128_exponent(x.bits()) >= B128_BIAS.sub(1)
    small: Bool = theta.is_zero() or theta.exponent < 0 - B128_TAN_LOOSE
    return reduced and small

### The fast tier's tan x before rounding, from |x| = k π/2 + θ with θ nonzero.
### :param x: The argument (its sign is applied at the end).
### :param red: The reduction of |x|.
### :returns: tan x at 192 bits.
secret routine wide_tan(x: B128, red: Reduced) -> B128Extended
    s: B128Extended = wide_sin_core(red.theta)
    c: B128Extended = wide_cos_core(red.theta)

    odd: Bool = red.quadrant & 1 == 1
    t: B128Extended = when
        odd => -(c / s)
        else => s / c

    # tan(θ + π/2) = -cot θ

    # tan is odd, and the reduction took |x|
    negative: Bool = b128_is_negative_bits(x.bits())
    r: B128Extended = when
        negative => -t
        else => t
    return r

### The exact tier: the same formulas in B128Big, rounded once.
### :param x: The argument, finite.
### :returns: tan x, correctly rounded.
secret routine big_tan(x: B128) -> B128
    red: BigReduced = big_reduce(x)
    s: B128Big = big_sin_core(red.theta)
    c: B128Big = big_cos_core(red.theta)

    odd: Bool = red.quadrant & 1 == 1
    t: B128Big = when
        odd => -(c / s)
        else => s / c
    negative: Bool = b128_is_negative_bits(x.bits())
    v: B128Big = when
        negative => -t
        else => t
    r: B128 = v.to[B128]()
    return r

### The quick tier: sin |x| and cos |x| from CORE-MATH's fast path, sharing one reduction, and their quotient
### rounded when it is far enough from a rounding midpoint.
### :param bits: The bits of x, finite with |x| >= 2^-57.
### :returns: The bits of tan x, correctly rounded.
### :absent: When the fast path can't vouch for its reduction, or the quotient lies within {B128_TAN_GATE} units
###     of a midpoint.
secret routine cm_tan_quick(bits: U128) -> Maybe[U128]
    magnitude: U128 = bits & B128_SIGN.bitnot()
    m: U128 = cm_trig_significand(magnitude)
    e: S64 = cm_trig_exponent(magnitude)

    small: Bool = e < -8
    values2: (CmTrigFast, CmTrigFast) = when
        small =>
            # below 2^-8 the argument is its own reduced angle
            sine: CmTrigFast = cm_trig_fast(m, e, cosine: false)
            becomes (sine, cm_trig_fast(m, e, cosine: true))
        else =>
            red: CmTrigReduced = cm_trig_reduce(m, e)
            if not red.ok
                # tan = sin / cos: the signs combine with the argument's, and the exponents subtract
                # the fast reduction kept too few bits (x near a multiple of π/256): the accurate path's 448-bit reduction
                # and 384-bit series, divided on their top 128 bits
                red2: CmTrigWideReduced = cm_trig_reduce_wide(m, e)
                series: CmTrigSeries = cm_trig_series(red2)
                one_minus: CmTrigU384 = cm_trig_one_minus(series.u)  # the sine or the cosine needs it
                sine: CmTrigWide = cm_trig_compose(red: red2, series, one_minus,
                    function: CmTrigFunction(cosine: false, sign: 0))
                cosine: CmTrigWide = cm_trig_compose(red: red2, series, one_minus,
                    function: CmTrigFunction(cosine: true, sign: 0))
                q: CmFracQuotient = cm_frac_quotient(s: sine.frac.l2, c: cosine.frac.l2)
                es: S64 = sine.exponent.to_wrap[S64]()
                ec: S64 = cosine.exponent.to_wrap[S64]()
                exponent: S64 = es - ec + q.scale
                trig_sign: U128 = sine.sign ^ cosine.sign
                result_sign: U128 = (bits & B128_SIGN) ^ trig_sign
                r: Maybe[U128] = cm_frac_round(frac: q.frac, exponent, sign: result_sign,
                    gate: B128_TAN_WIDE_GATE)
                return r
            t1: U128 = red.t1
            sq: CmTrigSquares = cm_trig_squares(t1, et: red.et)
            sine_theta: U128 = cm_trig_sin_frac(t1, u: sq.u, v: sq.v)
            sine: CmTrigFast = cm_trig_fast_tail(red, sq, sine_theta, cosine: false)
            becomes (sine, cm_trig_fast_tail(red, sq, sine_theta, cosine: true))
    sine, cosine = values2
    q: CmFracQuotient = cm_frac_quotient(s: sine.frac, c: cosine.frac)
    es: S64 = sine.exponent.to_wrap[S64]()
    ec: S64 = cosine.exponent.to_wrap[S64]()
    exponent: S64 = es - ec + q.scale
    trig_sign: U128 = sine.sign ^ cosine.sign
    result_sign: U128 = (bits & B128_SIGN) ^ trig_sign
    r: Maybe[U128] = cm_frac_round(frac: q.frac, exponent, sign: result_sign, gate: B128_TAN_GATE)
    return r
